  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1158 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nFriday through Wednesday:\n\nModels continue to show a signal for a period of showers to \nmove across the area Friday, as an area of focused warm air \nadvection and moisture in the low levels combines with a \nsouthwesterly low level jet nose pointing into the area. The \nsurface warm front also shifts northeast into the area during \nthis period, which may help with upward vertical motion. \n\nForecast soundings are trending more moist for a time Friday, as\nthese features move through. They show weak instability \ndeveloping as well, with a few hundred J/kg of elevated CAPE, \nsuggesting a few thunderstorms may be possible. Most ensemble \nmembers have light measurable rainfall during this time across \nthe area. Thus, continued to have 20 to 50 percent chances for \nshowers west to east across the area Friday.\n\nThe warm front will push north Friday night, with gusty south\nwinds on Saturday within a tight pressure gradient. This should\nbring very warm temperatures into the area, with highs possibly\nreaching around 80 degrees or into the lower 80s. Record highs\nare 84 degrees for Milwaukee and Madison, so those may be\napproached. \n\nAs the 500 mb ridge axis shifts east Saturday night into Sunday,\nany remnants with the tropical cyclone should remain southeast\nof the area, other than some clouds moving through. The clouds\nmay hold down high temperatures a bit on Sunday, along with \nsome onshore winds near the lake.\n\nThere still appears to be a cold front that shifts southeast\nthrough the area later Monday or Monday evening, with a 500 mb\nvorticity maximum within northwest flow shifting through the\nregion Tuesday. Ensemble members are fairly wet Monday into\nMonday night, so kept 20 to 50 percent chances for showers\ngoing. High pressure behind this system with some cold air\nadvection should bring more seasonable temperatures by the\nmiddle of next week.\n\nWood",
